So M-real is the actual manifold of physical, ecological, and social reality.

38:45.800 --> 38:47.040
It's the hard limits of the planet.

38:47.420 --> 38:52.040
It's the amount of wheat in the fields, the physical health of the workers, the amount of carbon in the atmosphere.

38:52.600 --> 38:54.700
It is the undeniable physical truth.

38:55.040 --> 38:55.880
And then there's M-proxy.

38:56.040 --> 38:56.180
Yes.

38:56.260 --> 38:58.820
M-proxy is the manifold of institutional metrics.

38:59.400 --> 39:05.480
This is the GDP, the stock market indices, standardized test scores in schools, social media engagement metrics.

39:05.480 --> 39:07.660
It's the dashboard we use to read the world.

39:07.900 --> 39:11.840
In a young, healthy civilization, M-proxy is tightly aligned with M-real.

39:12.280 --> 39:15.260
The dashboard metrics accurately reflect the physical reality.

39:15.720 --> 39:19.580
If the stock market goes up, it means more food is being grown and more houses are being built.

39:19.760 --> 39:22.860
But over time, optimization pressure causes drift.

39:23.180 --> 39:28.140
The math behind this is actually the rigorous geometric explanation of Goodhart's Law.

39:28.620 --> 39:29.320
Goodhart's Law.

39:29.820 --> 39:30.080
Right.

39:30.300 --> 39:31.220
Remind us what that is.

39:31.220 --> 39:36.200
Goodhart's Law states that when a measure becomes a target, it ceases to be a good measure.

39:36.480 --> 39:36.940
Exactly.

39:37.400 --> 39:40.400
And geometrically, here is why that happens.

39:40.680 --> 39:48.320
When we compress the infinite high-dimensional complexity of reality into a simple, single-number proxy metric,

39:48.680 --> 39:54.500
like GDP or a student's SAT score, we are performing a mathematical projection.

39:54.940 --> 39:57.820
